## Interface: 20506
## Title: WarriorKit
## Notes: Ctrl-click raid marking, casting on what the mouse is over with a key you bound by dragging the spell into a slot, a Charge button that aims by camera out of combat, a warrior bar loadout, threat-coloured enemy bars, damage healing and threat meters, a nag for the sharpening stone and the racial you forgot, a row counting down the cooldowns that decide fights, square class-coloured player and target frames, party and raid blocks ordered by role, your own cast bar, a square minimap with the addon buttons collected behind one square, a chat window of the addon's own with a room per conversation in place of the client's, groups for the people who matter and a voice channel joined at login, a loot stream and a combat log drawn as scrolling feeds with the addon's own tooltips on them, a filter for the red text in the middle of the screen, fast looting, trash selling, repairs paid for you, a longer camera, a carried Edit Mode layout and stripped bar art.
## Author: baudin
## IconTexture: Interface\AddOns\WarriorKit\Media\Icon.tga
## Version: 1.9
## SavedVariables: WarriorKitDB
## SavedVariablesPerCharacter: WarriorKitCharDB
## X-Category: Combat

# The fallback TOC, which is what the TBC Anniversary client (2.5.6, interface
# 20506) loads. WarriorKit_Vanilla.toc is the same file list for Classic Era.
# check.sh fails if the two lists ever drift apart.

# Core first. It creates ns.db, the API shims and the registry every feature
# signs into, and the panel walks that registry at PLAYER_LOGIN.
Core\Core.lua

# Where a Blizzard frame goes when this addon draws it instead: one frame,
# hidden at birth, that nothing can show. A frame re-parented into it is not
# drawn whatever the client calls on it, which is the guarantee ns.Strip in
# Core.lua cannot make on its own, because SetShown is resolved in C and never
# reads the Lua Show it replaced. Right after Core.lua, which owns ns.Strip and
# ns.Blocked, and above every part that takes one of the client's frames down.
Core\Attic.lua

# What the addon knows about a class, and one file per class that knows it.
# Class.lua is the registry and the question "which class is this"; each file
# beside it is the whole of what the addon knows about one class, and nothing
# outside that file names one of its spells. A class nobody has written a file
# for gets everything below that never asks. Loads before Core\Stance.lua and
# before every part, because all of them read facts off it and none of them
# reads one at load.
Class\Class.lua
Class\Warrior.lua
Class\Mage.lua
Class\Shaman.lua
Class\Priest.lua

# Two shared files that wrap a client API and own no setting, the same shape
# ns.ItemInfo has. Gear is what the client will let into each hand and which
# number that hand is; Stance reads your class's own form list off the registry
# above and hands back their localised names and which one you are standing in. Both were a part's private knowledge until
# a second part needed the same answer, and both load before anything that asks.
Core\Gear.lua
Core\Stance.lua

# The shared unit layer. What a mob or a group member is, asked once: the
# palette, the level tag, who is in the group, what they have spent their points
# on, which role that makes them and what the threat API says. Between Core and
# UI because none of it draws anything and everything that draws a unit wants
# it. Order inside the folder is dependency order: Color before Level, Roster
# before Threat, and Roster then Spec then Role, because Role asks the first two
# which unit a GUID is and which tree that unit took.
#
# Spec.lua was Meter\Spec.lua. It reads talent trees for you and for anyone the
# client will let you inspect, which is the only source of a real role on a
# client that has none, and a part may not name a file outside its own tree.
Unit\Unit.lua
Unit\Color.lua
Unit\Level.lua
Unit\Roster.lua
Unit\Spec.lua
Unit\Role.lua
Unit\Threat.lua

# The drawing layer, between Core and everything that draws. It owns the pixel
# grid, so it loads before the panel and before any part that puts a frame up.
UI\Pixel.lua
UI\Draw.lua

# The gauge, between the drawing primitives and anything that puts a bar up. A
# status bar with a flat fill and the spent part of it behind, which the enemy
# bars and the skinned unit frames both draw and both used to draw their own
# way. After UI\Draw.lua because it builds its underlay out of ns.Fill, and
# after Unit\Color.lua because the spent part is a fifth of the fill's colour
# and that fraction belongs to the unit palette.
UI\Gauge.lua

UI\Text.lua

# One aura, drawn: the cropped icon, the hairline round it, the sweep over the
# art, the time left and the stack count, and whether it is yours. The client's
# Cooldown frame draws the wedge, reversed, so an aura fills as it runs out
# where a cooldown empties. The debuff row on an enemy bar
# and the row under the skinned target block are both made of these. After
# UI\Draw.lua for the cropped icon and UI\Text.lua for the shared fonts, and
# after Unit\Color.lua, because what a debuff of yours looks like belongs to
# the unit palette and not to either row.
UI\Aura.lua

UI\Flow.lua

# One ability, drawn: the icon, the cooldown swipe, the timer, the count and
# the border that carries the status. After UI\Draw.lua for the cropped icon
# and UI\Text.lua for the shared fonts, and before every part that puts a
# castable square on the screen.
UI\Ability.lua

# The widget library, on top of that grid. Theme is the palette and the pixel
# metrics, Stack lays a column out and asks every row how tall it is, Scroll
# clips and scrolls, Widgets is the kit a page is built from, and Window is the
# chrome with its rail and its tab strip. All five load before Core\Panel.lua,
# which is the first thing that builds a page out of them.
UI\Theme.lua
UI\Stack.lua
UI\Scroll.lua

# A column of lines that grows from the bottom and scrolls, which is what a
# chat window is made of. After UI\Scroll.lua because it borrows that file's
# bar, and after UI\Theme.lua because it takes the palette into file-scope
# locals as it loads, the same as everything else on this layer.
UI\Log.lua

# The tooltip. Every hover in the addon went to GameTooltip until this existed,
# which meant a parchment scroll with a gold border rising out of an interface
# that has neither anywhere else. This is the addon's own: the theme's palette,
# the shared font, the pixel grid, and a scanner that reads an item's real text
# out of the client so a hovered item is drawn here rather than there. After
# UI\Theme.lua for the palette and UI\Text.lua for the fonts, and before every
# part that answers the mouse.
UI\Tooltip.lua

# A column of things that happened, newest at the top, older below it, scrolled
# by the wheel. It is not UI\Log.lua, which holds strings and cannot carry an
# icon per line, and it is not UI\Stack.lua, which relays the whole column every
# time a row arrives. After UI\Scroll.lua because it borrows that file's bar,
# and after UI\Tooltip.lua because a row's whole answer to the mouse is one.
UI\Feed.lua

UI\Widgets.lua
UI\Window.lua

Core\Command.lua
Core\Panel.lua

# One button in the client's own menu, which opens the panel above. After
# Core\Panel.lua because that is the file that owns the window it opens, and
# before every part because it belongs to the addon rather than to any of
# them.
Core\Menu.lua

# The parts. Within each, behaviour first and Feature.lua last, because
# Feature.lua is the only file that names anything outside its own folder.
# Perf first among the parts, because Charge/Icon.lua and the three other
# tickers call ns.Perf.Start by name. They resolve it at tick time rather than
# at load, so the order is belt and braces, and belt and braces is what a nil
# namespace on a ticker is worth: that is 1,189 errors in two minutes.
Perf\Perf.lua
Perf\Feature.lua

Marking\Marking.lua
Marking\Keys.lua
Marking\Feature.lua

# Casting on what the mouse is over, which is this addon's own Clique. Hover.lua
# is the model, Cast.lua is the secure button every key presses, Sheet.lua draws
# the list of what you bound over the world, Panel.lua is the page, and
# Feature.lua last as everywhere else. Cast and Sheet both read Hover, and
# nothing reads either of them but the page.
#
# After Marking, which is the other part built on a modified key and the thing
# under the cursor, and which is where the whole of the override binding
# reasoning is written down. It needs UI\Widgets.lua for the slot you drop a
# spell on and the pixel grid for the list, both of which are far above this
# line.
Hover\Hover.lua
Hover\Cast.lua
Hover\Sheet.lua
Hover\Panel.lua
Hover\Feature.lua

Charge\Charge.lua
Charge\Icon.lua
Charge\Marker.lua
Charge\SoftTarget.lua
Charge\Feature.lua

Targeting\Switch.lua
Targeting\Feature.lua

Loadouts\Loadouts.lua
Loadouts\Feature.lua

# Which of the two reactive abilities a slot is holding, and whether its window
# is open. Out of the combat log, warrior only, and read by Slot.lua on the
# bar's ticker, so it loads before it.
Buttons\Reaction.lua

# The other half of the same idea: what the fight has to have done before a
# press lands, for the abilities the client has no opinion about. Read off the
# target rather than out of the log, class data like the windows next door, and
# read by Slot.lua on the same tick, so it loads before it too.
Buttons\Requires.lua
Buttons\Slot.lua
Buttons\Layout.lua
Buttons\Ranks.lua

# The bars themselves, after the three files they read and before Feature.lua
# as everywhere else. Slot.lua says what one action slot is doing, Layout.lua
# resolves which slots a bar drives, and Bars.lua is what draws them, so it
# loads last of the three. It also needs UI\Ability.lua, UI\Flow.lua and the
# pixel grid, all of which are far above this line.
# Hiding the client's own action buttons, and putting them back. Its own file
# because it is the one part of the bars that touches a frame this addon does
# not own, and because how you hide a secure button without taking its taint
# with you is a subject rather than a detail. Before Bars.lua, which calls it.
Buttons\Blizzard.lua

# The plan of which bars this client can have, and the answer to which of them
# you want cloned. Before Bars.lua, which reads both at build time. Its own file
# because the answer stopped being a reading of the client the moment there was
# a tick box per bar, and because the plan is the one part of the bars that is
# source code rather than a setting.
Buttons\Which.lua

# What one bar looks like and when it is on the screen: the rows the twelve
# fold into, the colour and the opacity of the ground under them, and whether
# the bar goes down in combat or waits on a key. After Which.lua, whose plan
# holds the default shape of every bar, and before Bars.lua, which asks this
# file what to draw on every layout. Its own file because none of it knows what
# an action slot is and because two of the five answers are a state driver
# rather than a setting the addon can act on itself.
Buttons\Look.lua
Buttons\Bars.lua

# The trace, before the squares that print through it. Read only and off unless
# asked for: what the client says is under the cursor, and every gesture a
# square gets, for the drops on bar 1 that two fixes written from the code have
# now failed to explain.
Buttons\Trace.lua

# What one square answers to the mouse: the tooltip you get by hovering it and
# the drag that fills it. Split out of Bars.lua at the 800 line gate, and a real
# seam rather than a convenient one: everything in it hangs a script on a button
# and touches the cursor, and none of it knows what a bar is. After Bars.lua,
# which is the only caller, and after Layout.lua, whose probe it asks before it
# picks anything up.
Buttons\Square.lua

# Where each cloned bar sits and the handle you drag it by. After Bars.lua,
# which is the only caller, and separate from it because the bar's shape is
# decided by the plan in source and its position is the one thing you cannot
# sensibly type.
Buttons\Placing.lua
Buttons\Feature.lua

UnitFrames\Plates.lua

# The cast row one enemy bar carries, before the file that builds the bar. Its
# own file rather than forty more lines in EnemyBars.lua, and a real seam: it
# knows nothing about a plate, a list or a widget pool, and everything in it
# takes a widget and draws on it.
UnitFrames\Cast.lua

# Your own cast bar, after the file it takes every answer about a cast from and
# before nothing in particular: it draws on a frame of its own and no other file
# in this folder knows it exists. Cast.lua says whether there is a cast, how far
# along it is and what the seconds read; this places a bar under the swing timer
# and draws that on it, plus the one state a mob's cast bar has no use for,
# which is the cast that was interrupted.
UnitFrames\PlayerCast.lua

UnitFrames\EnemyBars.lua

# The target's own buff and debuff rows, before the file that builds the block
# they hang under. Its own file for the reason UnitFrames\Cast.lua is: it knows
# nothing about a portrait, a gauge or an Edit Mode anchor, and everything in it
# takes a skinned frame and draws two rows under it. It also hides the client's
# row, which is the half that cannot be a detail: leaving both up is two answers
# for where an aura goes.
UnitFrames\Auras.lua

# The switches that take Blizzard's own copy of something this addon draws off
# the screen: your buffs, your debuffs, the target's icons, the target's cast
# bar. One table of frames and one Apply, so adding the next one is a line
# rather than a file. Separate from Auras.lua because it answers on a client
# with no skin at all and knows nothing about a row.
UnitFrames\Blizzard.lua

# The skin, in four files. Skin.lua is the part: which of the three frames this
# client has, whether each one is wanted, and the order that styles and unstyles
# them without losing half a skin to combat. The three below it are the three
# subjects it used to hold, and each is loaded before the file that calls it.
#
# Everything the skin does to a region Blizzard owns: record it, hide it, hand
# it back. It knows nothing about a rectangle, and Block.lua reads the two lists
# in it rather than writing them out again.
UnitFrames\Art.lua

# The block itself: the square, the two rails, the four strings, and where the
# three blocks hang off each other. After Art.lua, whose kept-badge list it
# places, and after Auras.lua, whose rows it builds and lays out.
UnitFrames\Block.lua

# One pass over one block: the colours off the unit, the four numbers and the
# incoming heal. Its own file for the reason UnitFrames\Cast.lua is: it knows
# nothing about a walk, an anchor or an Edit Mode selection, and everything in
# it takes a block that already exists and writes what changed.
UnitFrames\Paint.lua

UnitFrames\Skin.lua

# One party or raid member's block: build it, lay it out, tick it. Its own file
# for the reason UnitFrames\Cast.lua is: it knows nothing about a header, an
# order or a setting other than the two sizes handed to it, and everything in it
# takes one button and draws on it. Before Group.lua, which is the only caller.
UnitFrames\Member.lua

# The party and raid header: its attributes, the slot order and where the block
# of frames sits. These are our frames rather than Blizzard's wearing our skin,
# because PartyMemberFrame1 is bound to party1 in XML and there is no supported
# way to point it at anyone else, so the moment the order is decided by role the
# client's frames cannot draw it. SecureGroupHeaderTemplate is the answer the
# client already ships: it makes one secure unit button per member and shows and
# hides them itself as the roster changes.
#
# After Unit\Role.lua, which decides the band each member sorts into, and after
# UnitFrames\Member.lua, which draws one of them. Both are far above this line.
UnitFrames\Group.lua

# The part's page in the options window, before Feature.lua, which is the only
# file that hands it to Core. Split out of Feature.lua at the 800 line gate,
# along the seam that file already had: everything in here reads a setting,
# draws a control and writes it back, and nothing in here decides anything.
UnitFrames\Panel.lua

UnitFrames\Feature.lua

# The meters. A damage and healing readout and a threat readout, two panes in
# one frame with no window round them. Two files that used to be here are under
# Unit\ now, both because a second part wanted the same answer: who is in the
# group is Unit\Roster.lua, because the enemy bars were walking the raid on
# their own ticker to get it, and which tree somebody took is Unit\Spec.lua,
# because the party frames sort by the role that tree makes them. Meter and
# Threat are independent of each other: one reads the combat log and one samples
# the client's threat API, and neither knows the other exists. Feature.lua last,
# as everywhere else.
Meter\Meter.lua
Meter\Threat.lua
Meter\Window.lua
Meter\Feature.lua

# The swing timer. Swing.lua is the clock, out of the combat log and
# UnitAttackSpeed; Slam.lua is where the press that costs no swing lands;
# Gauges.lua draws both hands and the band; Feature.lua last as everywhere
# else. Slam loads after Swing because a finished Slam restarts the swing and
# calls into it, and Gauges after both because it draws what the two answer.
# After the meters and before the art for no reason but the panel's order.
Swing\Swing.lua
Swing\Slam.lua
Swing\Gauges.lua
Swing\Feature.lua

# The buff nag. Upkeep.lua is what should be up and is not, out of your own
# auras and out of GetWeaponEnchantInfo; Racials.lua is the cooldown you own and
# have not pressed; Nag.lua draws the row; Feature.lua last as everywhere else.
# Racials loads after Upkeep for no reason but reading order, because neither
# knows the other exists and only Nag.lua reads both. After the swing timer
# because it is the other thing on screen that says what to press next, and it
# needs UI\Ability.lua, which is far above this line.
Buffs\Upkeep.lua
Buffs\Racials.lua
Buffs\Nag.lua
Buffs\Feature.lua

# The long cooldowns. Cooldowns.lua is what is on the row, out of your class's
# own list and the two trinket slots; Row.lua draws it; Feature.lua last as
# everywhere else. After the buff nag because it is the same question one step
# out, and it needs UI\Ability.lua, which is far above this line.
Cooldowns\Cooldowns.lua
Cooldowns\Row.lua
Cooldowns\Feature.lua

# The feeds. Two columns of what just happened: what dropped, and what landed on
# you. Stream.lua is the frame one of them sits in, its anchor and its settings,
# and it loads first because the other two ask it for one as they load. Loot and
# Combat share nothing with each other: one parses the client's loot sentences
# and one reads the combat log, and neither knows the other exists. Feature.lua
# last, as everywhere else.
#
# After Buffs because it is the last of the parts that draw over the world, and
# after Unit\Roster.lua, which is what the combat feed asks whether a GUID is
# one of yours. Both are far above this line.
#
# Purse.lua between the frame and the two feeds. It is the ledger behind the
# status strip along the bottom of the loot feed: what this character carries,
# what the account carries, and how fast that is moving. Before Loot.lua because
# that file folds the purse's defaults into its own as it loads.
Feeds\Stream.lua
Feeds\Purse.lua
# Auction.lua asks whichever auction addon the player has installed what an
# item goes for, and answers nothing at all where they have none. Nothing in
# the addon requires it and no TOC names one as a dependency: it is one line of
# one tooltip, and a player with no scanner is told nothing about that.
#
# Before Loot.lua because the loot feed's tooltip is the only thing that asks.
Feeds\Auction.lua
Feeds\Loot.lua
Feeds\Combat.lua
Feeds\Feature.lua

# The breakdown. What this character does, counted out of the combat log and
# kept between sessions: one row per ability, how often it lands, how often it
# crits, what stopped it and how much of your damage it is. Breakdown.lua counts
# and Window.lua draws the table into a window of its own, so Window loads after
# it, and Feature.lua last as everywhere else.
#
# It is not the meters and shares nothing with them. Meter/Meter.lua totals the
# pull you are in and forgets it when the next one starts; this never forgets
# and never reports a rate. Both read the same event and each registers its own
# handler, which is the house pattern: Swing, Reaction and the combat feed all
# do the same.
#
# After the feeds because it is the last thing in the addon that reads the
# combat log, and after Core\Panel.lua and the widget kit, which are far above
# this line, because the window is built out of the second and opened from the
# first.
Breakdown\Breakdown.lua
Breakdown\Window.lua
Breakdown\Feature.lua

Artwork\Artwork.lua
Artwork\Feature.lua

# The minimap. Shape.lua makes the frame square and as wide as you asked for,
# Corral.lua holds the other addons' buttons behind one square, and Feature.lua
# last as everywhere else. Shape first because the corral's default position is
# a corner of the map and the map has to have been resized before that corner
# means anything. Next to Artwork because it is the same job on another of
# Blizzard's frames: furniture off, everything reversible in one call.
Minimap\Shape.lua
Minimap\Clock.lua
Minimap\Corral.lua
Minimap\Feature.lua

# The chores. Nothing here is on the combat path, and the first four draw
# nothing at all, so they are independent of each other and of load order within
# the part. Clutter decides what is finished with and Destroy is the window that
# acts on it, so Clutter loads first. Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# Vendor and Repair both wake on MERCHANT_SHOW and share nothing else: one
# empties the bags and one pays for the mending, and neither knows the other
# exists. Vendor is listed first because it is the older of the two and for no
# other reason.
Comfort\Loot.lua
Comfort\Vendor.lua
Comfort\Repair.lua
Comfort\Camera.lua

# The error filter. Not a chore like the four above it, in that nothing runs
# for you; it is a list of messages you have decided you do not need, and one
# replaced method standing in front of the frame that would draw them. Here
# because Comfort is the part that owns the settings that belong to no feature,
# and because the list is one tab rather than a rail entry of its own.
Comfort\Errors.lua
Comfort\Clutter.lua
Comfort\Destroy.lua
Comfort\Feature.lua

# The social part. Who you play with, what they said and how you talk to them.
# People.lua holds the groups and Rooms.lua reads them to work out which
# conversation a line belongs to, so it loads before Feed.lua, which routes
# through it. Compose.lua is the line you type and knows only about channels.
# Blizzard.lua takes the client's own chat window off the screen and hands what
# it would have drawn to the feed. Voice.lua shares nothing with any of them: it
# is one setting and a channel to join. Window.lua draws what the other five
# produce, and Feature.lua is last, as everywhere else.
Chat\People.lua
Chat\Rooms.lua
Chat\Compose.lua
Chat\Feed.lua
Chat\Blizzard.lua
Chat\Voice.lua
Chat\Window.lua
Chat\Feature.lua

# Saved.lua is generated by bake-ui.sh and only assigns a table, so it loads
# after the module that owns the table and before the part that reads it.
EditMode\EditMode.lua
EditMode\Saved.lua
EditMode\Feature.lua

# The addon's own settings, which belong to no feature. Last, because the only
# one of them today is how big a window is drawn and it is applied at
# ADDON_LOADED, by which point every file in this list has run. Behaviour first
# and Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
Settings\Settings.lua
Settings\Feature.lua
